Fillets and chamfers whose contact runs along the far edge of a face: a
face exactly as wide as the rounding's or the chamfer's contact distance
(mitcad#121). The blend touches that face on its boundary all along, and
the face vanishes in the result.

- ChFi3d_Builder::SplitKPart: the tangency line of an analytic blend
  (plane, cylinder, cone, torus) is cut into the domains of its faces by
  hatching. A line lying on the boundary of its face has no domain inside
  the face for the hatcher: the split failed, the stripe went to the
  walking path (an approximated surface instead of the exact one), whose
  start on the boundary was refused, so that the blend failed. Such a line
  (HatchOnBoundary: no domain, and the same line moved into the face by
  ten times the 2d tolerance has domains along which the line lies on the
  boundary within Precision::Confusion) now takes the domains of the moved
  line; the ends it finds on the arcs across, near a vertex, are put at
  that vertex (SnapToVertex: on the line at the vertex, on the arc at the
  vertex's parameter).
- ChFi3d_ContactsAlongFaceEdges (new, called in ChFi3d_Builder::Compute
  before ChFi3d_FilDS, after the corners): a surface data whose contact
  curve on a face covers a whole edge of that face (its ends at the edge's
  vertices, its points on the edge within a tenth of the 3d approximation
  tolerance) is stored as a blend on that edge, the form 0011 gives a
  blend rolling on an obstacle edge: that side takes the face beyond the
  edge (its p-curve on that face, the transition seen from it) and the
  edge as its curve. ChFi3d_CutConsumedFaces then drops the face it
  consumes; before, the builder trimmed that face to a face of no area
  along the edge (or an invalid wire). A blend made a little smaller than
  its face (a retry at 99.9 %) stays inside the face.

A contact that runs along an edge for part of the blend only is left as
it was, and so is a walked (non-analytic) blend whose contact runs along
the boundary: its start there is still refused.
